{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-39647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,2$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n18,\\,12,\\,8,\\,\\frac{16}{3},\\,…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=18$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{12}{18}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,2.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ 18{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,2 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,2}{18} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,011)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}+1≈29.159\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $30$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39647" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "31429944cc5d33f24227ab71ab288a42cdc2a672fcb434232d181c4b85433e5b"} {"question" "q-39647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $28$th term"] ["text" "the $29$th term"] ["text" "the $30$th term"] ["text" "the $31$st term"] ["text" "the $33$rd term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $30$th term"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the first term that is less than $0.001$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,48,\\,36,\\,27,\\,…\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{48}{64}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.001 \\\\ 64{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <0.001 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.001}{64} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,016)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}+1≈39.386\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $40$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "31429944cc5d33f24227ab71ab288a42cdc2a672fcb434232d181c4b85433e5b"} {"question" "q-39648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $38$th term"] ["text" "the $39$th term"] ["text" "the $40$th term"] ["text" "the $41$th term"] ["text" "the $42$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39648" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $40$th term"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $75$, and the common ratio is $5.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,713?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=75$ and  $r=5$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{3-1} \\\\ 75 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ 75 & =25{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,713.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{n-1} & >1\\,713 \\\\ {5}^{n-1} & >571 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{5}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(571) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(571) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1≈4.944\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,713.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39651" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "31429944cc5d33f24227ab71ab288a42cdc2a672fcb434232d181c4b85433e5b"} {"question" "q-39651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the $3$rd term"] ["text" "the $4$th term"] ["text" "the $5$th term"] ["text" "the $6$th term"] ["text" "the $7$th term"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-39651" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the $5$th term"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-278083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The $5$th term of a geometric sequence is $81$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $10\\,000$ is the{{field-1}} th term." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-278083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given that ${a}_{5}=81$ and  $r=\\frac{3}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{5-1} \\\\ 81 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4} \\\\ 81 & =\\frac{81}{16}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $10\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >10\\,000 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >625 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(625) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(625) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(625)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈16.877.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $17$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $10\\,000.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-278083" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "31429944cc5d33f24227ab71ab288a42cdc2a672fcb434232d181c4b85433e5b"} {"question" "q-278083" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-278083" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "17"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-278089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence with a positive common ratio is $2\\,304$, and the $6$th term is $36\\,864.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $496\\,800$ is the{{field-1}} th term." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-278089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and ${a}_{6}=36\\,864$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{36\\,864}{2\\,304} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ r & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=2\\,304$ and  $r=4$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{4-1} \\\\ 2\\,304 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,304 & =64{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =36\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $496\\,800.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >496\\,800 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >13\\,800 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(13\\,800)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈7.876.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $496\\,800.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-278089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "31429944cc5d33f24227ab71ab288a42cdc2a672fcb434232d181c4b85433e5b"} {"question" "q-278089" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067996/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a0ac5e2651585c824544a2ffaf666766588c2cf33552d9f7f8eef93743a6f7f"} {"question" "q-278089" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answe